---
# yamllint disable rule:line-length
# Strips the debug line-number entries jeo emits when disassembling in debug
# mode (a Φ.jeo.line-number per statement boundary). They are pure
# LineNumberTable bookkeeping with no stack effect, so dropping them leaves
# the bytecode semantically identical — the optimised class simply carries no
# line-number table.
#
# The pattern is not scoped to a method, and cannot be: a rule matches a
# subtree, while "this method holds a pipeline" is a fact about the whole
# method body. So the class loses its line numbers everywhere, including the
# methods no later rule touches, and a stack trace through them reads
# "Foo.java" with no line (see #1084). A class with no stream operator at all
# is spared, because grep-in skips it before any rule runs.
#
# A project that would rather keep its stack traces can leave this rule out and
# take the rest, since the selection grammar supports negation:
#
#   <rules>streams/*,!streams/1xx/151-*</rules>
#
# The later rules then see a line-number between the opcodes they reason about,
# and fuse fewer pipelines.
#
# Labels are deliberately left intact: a label may be a jump, a frame, a
# try-catch boundary or a local-variable-table scope target, and deciding
# that locally is not possible, so removing one is unsafe. Only the
# line-numbers go.
#
# Done first, as a 1xx prep step, this keeps the only debug entry that can
# appear anywhere out of the way of later rules that reason about opcode
# adjacency (an own-line `.skip(n)` is left with just a bare label between the
# count-push and the call, which 219 removes, rather than a label plus a
# line-number).
name: remove-line-numbers
pattern: |
  ⟦
    𝐵-before,
    𝜏-line ↦ ⟦ φ ↦ Φ.jeo.line-number, 𝐵-line ⟧,
    𝐵-after
  ⟧
result: |
  ⟦
    𝐵-before,
    𝐵-after
  ⟧
